WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.574 --> 00:00:03.324
(dramatic music)

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.830
- How did America rise up
from a back woods country

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.753
to be one of the greatest
nations the world has ever known?

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.473
We pioneered industries.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.340
And all this required
the greatest innovations

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.973
in science and technology in the world.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.830
And so, science is a fundamental part

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.413
of the country that we are.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.400
But, in this the 21st century

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.740
when it comes time to make
decisions about science

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.880
it seems to me people
have lost the ability

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.100
to judge what is true and what is not.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.860
What is reliable and what is not reliable?

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.700
What should you believe and
what should you not believe?

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.700
And, when you have people who
don't know much about science,

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.393
standing in denial of
it, and rising to power,

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.560
that is a recipe for
the complete dismantling

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.183
of our informed democracy.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.060
- Let us demand, that
educators around America

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.640
teach evolution, not
as fact, but as theory.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.200
- [Reporter] Increasing number of parents

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.660
showing skepticism about vaccinations.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.440
- [Reporter] Voters have
approved a ban on GMO's.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.193
- [Reporter] Call climate
change, unproven science.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.913
- It's not the country I
remember growing up in.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.250
Not that we didn't have challenges.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.420
I'm old enough to remember
the 60s and the 70s.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.110
Got a hot war and a Cold War,
the Civil Rights movement,

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.583
and all this was going on,

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.320
but I don't remember any time

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.733
where people were standing in
denial of what science was.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.170
One of the great things
about science, is that,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.370
it is an entire exercise

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.533
in finding what is true.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.070
You have a hypothesis you
test it, I get a result,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.740
a rival of mine, double checks it.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.230
Because they think I might be wrong,

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.300
they perform an even better
experiment than I did

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.453
and they find out, hey,
this experiment matches.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.653
Oh my gosh, we're on to something here.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.790
And out of this rises
a new emergent truth.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.760
It does it better than anything else

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.973
we have ever come up with as human beings.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.323
This is science.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.263
It's not something to toy with.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.640
It's not something to say
I choose not to be believe

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.373
E=MC squared, you don't have that option.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.260
When you have an established
scientific emergent truth

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.520
it is true, whether or
not you believe in it,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.563
and the sooner you understand that,

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.240
the faster we can get on with
the political conversations

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.253
about how to solve the
problems that face us.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.290
So once you understand that

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.630
humans are warming the planet,

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:24.910
you can then have a political
conversation about that.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:27.340
You can say, well should we,
are there carbon credits?

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.850
Do we do this, do we put a tariff on this?

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.500
Do we fund, do we subsidize?

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.200
Those have political answers.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.370
And every minute one is
in denial you are delaying

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.980
the political solution that should

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.374
have been established years ago.

00:03:44.374 --> 00:03:47.124
(dramatic music)

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.490
As a voter, as a citizen,
scientific issues

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:57.247
will come before you and,
isn't it worth it to say,

00:03:57.247 --> 00:03:59.547
"All right, at least let me
become scientifically literate

00:03:59.547 --> 00:04:01.697
"so that I can think about these issues

00:04:01.697 --> 00:04:04.313
"and act intelligently upon them."

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.660
Recognize what science is

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:13.660
and allow it to be what
it can and should be

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.493
in the service of civilization.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:18.843
It's in our hands.

